            <ArticleTitle>Coronary subclavian steal syndrome detected during coronary bypass surgery in a hemodialysis patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>154-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/jocs.12444</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Coronary subclavian steal syndrome associated with upper extremity arteriovenous fistula is an uncommon but potentially life-threatening condition. We present a case of a 65-year-old male on hemodialysis with a left upper extremity arteriovenous fistula who underwent coronary revascularization involving bypass with the left internal thoracic artery to the left anterior descending artery. Intraoperative transit-time graft flow measurements and fluorescence imaging showed a reversed flow in the left internal thoracic artery, and the left internal thoracic artery was successfully converted as a free graft from the ascending aorta to the left anterior descending artery.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2014 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
